    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62502,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 516,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Symptoms are related to the presence of an adnexal mass {21778097; 20871224; 21979589; 28463908}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:33:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62503,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 516,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>This is a rare tumour, with insufficient epidemiological data available. Only a few case reports have been published {20871224; 21778097; 21979589; 28463908}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:33:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62504,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 516,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:33:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62505,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 516,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>CTNNB1</em> mutations are present in most tumours {28463908}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:33:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62506,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 516,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumours range in size, but most are &gt;&nbsp;5&nbsp;cm (up to 25&nbsp;cm), with an intact external surface. On sectioning, they are characteristically solid and cystic; the cystic areas are friable and yellow to tan. They may show areas of haemorrhage {20871224; 21979589; 28463908}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:33:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62507,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 516,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>These tumours show three main histological patterns: solid (sometimes punctuated by cysts), nests (separated by stroma, which may be conspicuously hyalinized), and pseudopapillae (due to dyscohesion; sometimes with myxoid to hyalinized cores). Cells are polygonal, with cytoplasm that varies from eosinophilic to pale and foamy with frequent paranuclear vacuoles. Intracellular and extracellular hyaline globules may be seen. Nuclei are round (sometimes with grooves) and cytologically bland. There is typically minimal or no mitotic activity. There may be an antipodal distribution of the nuclei at the base of the papillae. The stroma may contain thin arborizing blood vessels. Tumour cells are usually positive for vimentin, CD10, CD56, CD99, WT1, &beta;-catenin (nuclear and cytoplasmic), and &alpha;1-antitrypsin, and they may stain for CAM5.2 (sometimes dot-like perinuclear), PR, synaptophysin, and KIT (CD117). Chromogranin, calretinin, and inhibin are negative. There is loss of membranous E-cadherin expression {20871224; 21979589; 28463908}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-23T15:59:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62508,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 516,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
